and update your distributions to include this new version.


I have just released another version of procps, the suite of ps-type
programs that uses the /proc filesystem, to
tsx-11.mit.edu:/pub/linux/BETA/procps/procps-0.8.tar.gz and
sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/Incoming/procps-0.8.tar.gz

This is a large suite of programs, including ps, free, uptime, w,
fuser, killall, top, tload, xload, and many more, all of which use the
/proc filesystem to gather their data.  Previous versions of these
programs needed to read kernel memory (/dev/kmem) and therefore had to
be either setuid root or setgid kmem, but the procps utilities do not
need to be setuid at all, thus avoiding security holes.

With procps, it is not necessary to recompile ps with new kernel
releases, and it is not necessary to re-port ps every few releases.
Therefore, for most users, procps is the recommended version of ps to
install.


New in this release:

The "TT" field has been expanded; it is now called "TTY", and takes
three characters instead of two.  Virtual consoles are labeled "v01"
and so on, serial ports are labeled "s00" and so on, and pty's are
labeled "p00" and so on.  Console is now "con" instead of "co".

Top now supports the `q' command line option, thanks to Joel Hoffman.

psmisc.0.5 has been included, so killall, fuser, and pstree are
updated to the latest versions.

A man page for psupdate has been included, and psupdate now updates
the zSystem file by default, since uncompressed kernels are no longer
supported.

The diagnostic message that ps spits out if /proc is not mounted has
been reformatted a little to be easier to read.

ps now munges the command lines so that weird characters in the
command line, like \n et. al. don't mess up the output.

w re-fixed (a patch I added for version 0.7 broke a previous fix of
the same problem) to print 8 character usernames properly on all systems.

Other little fixes I've forgotten.

michaelkjohnson

