>In article <1993Sep6.111658.18252@mnemosyne.cs.du.edu> mcodogno@nyx.cs.du.edu (Maurizio Codogno) writes:
>>
>>If I correctly checked, there is no "noauto" option in fstab code
>>(on SunOs and I think BSDish systems, it says not to mount the
>>filesystem automatically with `mount -a'.
>>I think it is a very handy option to set parameters for floppies
>>without the system trying to search for them at boot. 
>>Of course it is not that difficult to add the option to the 
>>mount sources, and I think I'll eventually do so; I just wonder
>>if it is just a bell&whistle or it could be useful to other people.
>>Any comments?
>
>No, it's definately useful for floppy disks, CD's, etc.
>
>At one point, one of the Linux mount programs had support for -noauto,
>but it was removed.  Having noauto lines in my mount file, I added it 
>to the .99.6 version of mount, I don't know what the code looks like to
>newer versions of mount but I could probably get patches.

I've implemented this for the usermount code.  The patches below are against
usermount-0.3, which can be found on sunsite.unc.edu:/pub/Linux/system/Misc/
mount/usermount-0.3.tar.gz.

The patches also implement the remount option to make it possible to change
the options of an already-mounted filesystem, and implement a proper sequence
for umount -a (namely, the reverse of what's found in mtab).  See the README
file (which is also patched :-) for a list of my changes.

Please let me know if you find any bugs or if you have any suggestions for
enhancements.

BTW, the umount supplied with this package isn't capable of unmounting the
root filesystem.  This could be a problem with e2fs.  If someone would be so
kind as to tell me how to implement this (or point me to code that does), I'll
be happy to do so.

Here are the patches.  Apply by going into the directory where your usermount
code is and do a "patch -p" with the following as standard input:

